  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Porodisculus pendulus is a small, delicate fungus belonging to the Fistulinaceae family, characterized by its distinctive pore-bearing undersurface. This rare species is found in the moist forests of New England, where it plays an important ecological role in wood decomposition.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Pore Surface",
      "description": "Tiny, angular pores on the underside rather than gills, which is diagnostic for the Fistulinaceae family",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Small Size",
      "description": "Fruiting bodies are notably small and delicate compared to related species",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Pendant Growth",
      "description": "The species name 'pendulus' refers to its hanging or suspended growth habit on decaying wood",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Porodisculus pendulus is found on decaying hardwood, particularly in cool, moist deciduous and mixed forests throughout New England.",
